n event object for the current device using cite136†cudaEventDefault .\\nL271: \\nL272: Note:\\nL273: \\nL274:   * Note that this function may also return error codes from previous, asynchronous launches.\\nL275: \\nL276:   * Note that this function may also return cite137†cudaErrorInitializationError , cite138†cudaErrorInsufficientDriver or cite139†cudaErrorNoDevice if this call tries to initialize internal CUDA RT state.\\nL277:   * Note that as specified by cite140†cudaStreamAddCallback no CUDA function may be called from callback. cite141†cudaErrorNotPermitted may, but is not guaranteed to, be returned as a diagnostic in such case.\\nL278: \\nL279: See also:\\nL280: \\nL281: cite142†cudaEventCreate ( C++ API) , cite124†cudaEventCreateWithFlags , cite128†cudaEventRecord , cite127†cudaEventQuery , cite131†cudaEventSynchronize , cite125†cudaEventDestroy , cite126†cudaEventElapsedTime , cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent , cite144†cuEventCreate L282: __host__ ​ __device__ ​cite121†cudaError_t cudaEventCreateWithFlags ( cite123†cudaEvent_t * event, unsigned int  flags )\\nL283: \\nL284: \\nL285: Creates an event object with the specified flags.\\nL286: ###### Parameters\\nL287: \\nL288: `event`\\nL289:     - Newly created event\\nL290: `flags`\\nL291:     - Flags for new event\\nL292: \\nL293: ###### Returns\\nL294: \\nL295: cite132†cudaSuccess , cite133†cudaErrorInvalidValue , cite134†cudaErrorLaunchFailure , cite135†cudaErrorMemoryAllocation L296: ###### Description\\nL297: \\nL298: Creates an event object for the current device with the specified flags. Valid flags include:\\nL299: \\nL300:   * cite136†cudaEventDefault : Default event creation flag.\\nL301: \\nL302:   * cite145†cudaEventBlockingSync : Specifies that event should use blocking synchronization. A host thread that uses cite131†cudaEventSynchronize() to wait on an event created with this flag will block until the event actually completes.\\nL303:   * cite146†cudaEventDisableTiming : Specifies that the created event does not need to record timing data. Events created with this flag specified and the cite145†cudaEventBlockingSync flag not specified will provide the best performance when used with cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent() and cite127†cudaEventQuery() .\\nL304:   * cite147†cudaEventInterprocess : Specifies that the created event may be used as an interprocess event by cite148†cudaIpcGetEventHandle() . cite147†cudaEventInterprocess must be specified along with cite146†cudaEventDisableTiming .\\nL305: \\nL306: Note:\\nL307: \\nL308:   * Note that this function may also return error codes from previous, asynchronous launches.\\nL309:   * Note that this function may also return cite137†cudaErrorInitializationError , cite138†cudaErrorInsufficientDriver or cite139†cudaErrorNoDevice if this call tries to initialize internal CUDA RT state.\\nL310: \\nL311:   * Note that as specified by cite140†cudaStreamAddCallback no CUDA function may be called from callback. cite141†cudaErrorNotPermitted may, but is not guaranteed to, be returned as a diagnostic in such case.\\nL312: \\nL313: See also:\\nL314: cite122†cudaEventCreate ( C API) , cite131†cudaEventSynchronize , cite125†cudaEventDestroy , cite126†cudaEventElapsedTime , cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent , cite144†cuEventCreate L315: \\nL316: __host__ ​ __device__ ​cite121†cudaError_t cudaEventDestroy ( cite123†cudaEvent_t event )\\nL317: \\nL318: \\nL319: Destroys an event object.\\nL320: ###### Parameters\\nL321: \\nL322: `event`\\nL323:     - Event to destroy\\nL324: \\nL325: ###### Returns\\nL326: \\nL327: cite132†cudaSuccess , cite133†cudaErrorInvalidValue , cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle , cite134†cudaErrorLaunchFailure L328: ###### Description\\nL329: \\nL330: Destroys the event specified by `event`.\\nL331: \\nL332: An event may be destroyed before it is complete (i.e., while cite127†cudaEventQuery() would return cite150†cudaErrorNotReady ). In this case, the call does not block on completion of the event, and any associated resources will automatically be released asynchronously at completion.\\nL333: \\nL334: Note:\\nL335: \\nL336:   * Note that this function may also return error codes from previous, asynchronous launches.\\nL337:   * Note that this function may also return cite137†cudaErrorInitializationError , cite138†cudaErrorInsufficientDriver or cite139†cudaErrorNoDevice if this call tries to initialize internal CUDA RT state.\\nL338: \\nL339:   * Note that as specified by cite140†cudaStreamAddCallback no CUDA function may be called from callback. cite141†cudaErrorNotPermitted may, but is not guaranteed to, be returned as a diagnostic in such case.\\nL340: \\nL341:   * Use of the handle after this call is undefined behavior.\\nL342:   * Returns cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle in the event of being passed NULL as the input event.\\nL343: \\nL344: See also:\\nL345: \\nL346: cite122†cudaEventCreate ( C API) , cite124†cudaEventCreateWithFlags , cite127†cudaEventQuery , cite131†cudaEventSynchronize , cite128†cudaEventRecord , cite126†cudaEventElapsedTime , cite151†cuEventDestroy L347: \\nL348: __host__ ​cite121†cudaError_t cudaEventElapsedTime ( float* ms, cite123†cudaEvent_t start, cite123†cudaEvent_t end )\\nL349: \\nL350: \\nL351: Computes the elapsed time between events.\\nL352: ###### Parameters\\nL353: \\nL354: `ms`\\nL355:     - Time between `start` and `end` in ms\\nL356: `start`\\nL357:     - Starting event\\nL358: `end`\\nL359:     - Ending event\\nL360: \\nL361: ###### Returns\\nL362: \\nL363: cite132†cudaSuccess , cite150†cudaErrorNotReady , cite133†cudaErrorInvalidValue , cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle , cite134†cudaErrorLaunchFailure , cite152†cudaErrorUnknown L364: ###### Description\\nL365: \\nL366: Computes the elapsed time between two events (in milliseconds with a resolution of around 0.5 microseconds). Note this API is not guaranteed to return the latest errors for pending work. As such this API is intended to serve as a elapsed time calculation only and polling for completion on the events to be compared should be done with cite127†cudaEventQuery instead.\\nL367: If either event was last recorded in a non-NULL stream, the resulting time may be greater than expected (even if both used the same stream handle). This happens because the cite128†cudaEventRecord() operation takes place asynchronously and there is no guarantee that the measured latency is actually just between the two events. Any number of other different stream operations could execute in between the two measured events, thus altering the timing in a significant way.\\nL368: If cite128†cudaEventRecord() has not been called on either event, then cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle is returned. If cite128†cudaEventRecord() has been called on both events but one or both of them has not yet been completed (that is, cite127†cudaEventQuery() would return cite150†cudaErrorNotReady on at least one of the events), cite150†cudaErrorNotReady is returned. If either event was created with the cite146†cudaEventDisableTiming flag, then this function will return cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle .\\nL369: Note:\\nL370: \\nL371:   * Note that this function may also return error codes from previous, asynchronous launches.\\nL372: \\nL373:   * Note that this function may also return cite137†cudaErrorInitializationError , cite138†cudaErrorInsufficientDriver or cite139†cudaErrorNoDevice if this call tries to initialize internal CUDA RT state.\\nL374: \\nL375:   * Note that as specified by cite140†cudaStreamAddCallback no CUDA function may be called from callback. cite141†cudaErrorNotPermitted may, but is not guaranteed to, be returned as a diagnostic in such case.\\nL376:   * Returns cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle in the event of being passed NULL as the input event.\\nL377: \\nL378: See also:\\nL379: \\nL380: cite122†cudaEventCreate ( C API) , cite124†cudaEventCreateWithFlags , cite127†cudaEventQuery , cite131†cudaEventSynchronize , cite125†cudaEventDestroy , cite128†cudaEventRecord , cite153†cuEventElapsedTime L381: \\nL382: __host__ ​cite121†cudaError_t cudaEventQuery ( cite123†cudaEvent_t event )\\nL383: \\nL384: \\nL385: Queries an event's status.\\nL386: ###### Parameters\\nL387: \\nL388: `event`\\nL389:     - Event to query\\nL390: \\nL391: ###### Returns\\nL392: \\nL393: cite132†cudaSuccess , cite150†cudaErrorNotReady , cite133†cudaErrorInvalidValue , cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle , cite134†cudaErrorLaunchFailure L394: ###### Description\\nL395: \\nL396: Queries the status of all work currently captured by `event`. See cite128†cudaEventRecord() for details on what is captured by an event.\\nL397: \\nL398: Returns cite132†cudaSuccess if all captured work has been completed, or cite150†cudaErrorNotReady if any captured work is incomplete.\\nL399: \\nL400: For the purposes of Unified Memory, a return value of cite132†cudaSuccess is equivalent to having called cite131†cudaEventSynchronize() .\\nL401: \\nL402: Note:\\nL403:   * Note that this function may also return error codes from previous, asynchronous launches.\\nL404: \\nL405:   * Note that this function may also return cite137†cudaErrorInitializationError , cite138†cudaErrorInsufficientDriver or cite139†cudaErrorNoDevice if this call tries to initialize internal CUDA RT state.\\nL406: \\nL407:   * Note that as specified by cite140†cudaStreamAddCallback no CUDA function may be called from callback. cite141†cudaErrorNotPermitted may, but is not guaranteed to, be returned as a diagnostic in such case.\\nL408:   * Returns cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle in the event of being passed NULL as the input event.\\nL409: \\nL410: See also:\\nL411: \\nL412: cite122†cudaEventCreate ( C API) , cite124†cudaEventCreateWithFlags , cite128†cudaEventRecord , cite131†cudaEventSynchronize , cite125†cudaEventDestroy , cite126†cudaEventElapsedTime , cite154†cuEventQuery L413: \\nL414: __host__ ​ __device__ ​cite121†cudaError_t cudaEventRecord ( cite123†cudaEvent_t event, cite129†cudaStream_t stream = 0 )\\nL415: \\nL416: \\nL417: Records an event.\\nL418: ###### Parameters\\nL419: \\nL420: `event`\\nL421:     - Event to record\\nL422: `stream`\\nL423:     - Stream in which to record event\\nL424: \\nL425: ###### Returns\\nL426: \\nL427: cite132†cudaSuccess , cite133†cudaErrorInvalidValue , cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle , cite134†cudaErrorLaunchFailure L428: ###### Description\\nL429: \\nL430: Captures in `event` the contents of `stream` at the time of this call. `event` and `stream` must be on the same CUDA context. Calls such as cite127†cudaEventQuery() or cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent() will then examine or wait for completion of the work that was captured. Uses of `stream` after this call do not modify `event`. See note on default stream behavior for what is captured in the default case.\\nL431: cite128†cudaEventRecord() can be called multiple times on the same event and will overwrite the previously captured state. Other APIs such as cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent() use the most recently captured state at the time of the API call, and are not affected by later calls to cite128†cudaEventRecord() . Before the first call to cite128†cudaEventRecord() , an event represents an empty set of work, so for example cite127†cudaEventQuery() would return cite132†cudaSuccess .\\nL432: \\nL433: Note:\\nL434:   * This function uses standard cite155†default stream semantics.\\nL435: \\nL436:   * Note that this function may also return error codes from previous, asynchronous launches.\\nL437: \\nL438:   * Note that this function may also return cite137†cudaErrorInitializationError , cite138†cudaErrorInsufficientDriver or cite139†cudaErrorNoDevice if this call tries to initialize internal CUDA RT state.\\nL439:   * Note that as specified by cite140†cudaStreamAddCallback no CUDA function may be called from callback. cite141†cudaErrorNotPermitted may, but is not guaranteed to, be returned as a diagnostic in such case.\\nL440: \\nL441:   * Returns cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle in the event of being passed NULL as the input event.\\nL442: \\nL443: See also:\\nL444: cite122†cudaEventCreate ( C API) , cite124†cudaEventCreateWithFlags , cite127†cudaEventQuery , cite131†cudaEventSynchronize , cite125†cudaEventDestroy , cite126†cudaEventElapsedTime , cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent , cite130†cudaEventRecordWithFlags , cite156†cuEventRecord L445: \\nL446: __host__ ​cite121†cudaError_t cudaEventRecordWithFlags ( cite123†cudaEvent_t event, cite129†cudaStream_t stream = 0, unsigned int  flags = 0 )\\nL447: \\nL448: \\nL449: Records an event.\\nL450: ###### Parameters\\nL451: \\nL452: `event`\\nL453:     - Event to record\\nL454: `stream`\\nL455:     - Stream in which to record event\\nL456: `flags`\\nL457:     - Parameters for the operation(See above)\\nL458: \\nL459: ###### Returns\\nL460: \\nL461: cite132†cudaSuccess , cite133†cudaErrorInvalidValue , cite149†cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle , cite134†cudaErrorLaunchFailure L462: ###### Description\\nL463: \\nL464: Captures in `event` the contents of `stream` at the time of this call. `event` and `stream` must be on the same CUDA context. Calls such as cite127†cudaEventQuery() or cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent() will then examine or wait for completion of the work that was captured. Uses of `stream` after this call do not modify `event`. See note on default stream behavior for what is captured in the default case.\\nL465: cite130†cudaEventRecordWithFlags() can be called multiple times on the same event and will overwrite the previously captured state. Other APIs such as cite143†cudaStreamWaitEvent() use the most recently captured state at the time of the API call, and are not affected by later calls to cite130†cudaEventRecordWithFlags() . Before the first call to cite130†cudaEventRecordWithFlags() , an event represents an empty set of work, so for example cite127†cudaEventQuery() would return cite132†cudaSuccess .\\nL466: \\nL467: flags include:\\nL468:   * cite157†cudaEventRecordDefault : Default event creation flag.\\nL469: \\nL470:   * cite158†cudaEventRecordExternal : Event is captured in the graph as an external event node when performing stream capture.\\nL471: \\nL472: Note:\\nL473: \\nL474:   * This function uses standard cite155†default stream semantics.\\nL475: \\nL476:   * Note that this function may also return error codes from previous, asynchronous launches.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n2.5. Asynchronous Execution — CUDA Programming Guide (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/02-basics/asynchronous-execution.html)\\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn1view0\\\",\\\"id\\\":11}); Total lines: 564\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08ac9-cff2-7973-a70d-a56359932a83","create_time":1789035214.07194}}}
